WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.000
One of the things that we've been talking
about is how the political discourse has coarsened

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.929
under Donald Trump, but the roots of this
actually predate Trump by quite a bit.

00:00:08.929 --> 00:00:09.929
Yeah.

00:00:09.929 --> 00:00:11.780
Trump is the anti intellectual president.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:16.910
He's coalesced one of the least, uh, least
educated and most uninformed elements of the

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:17.910
electorate.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:23.220
For his benefit, but that was already starting
to happen under George W. Bush decrying education

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:26.449
as liberal indoctrination has gone back for
a while.

00:00:26.449 --> 00:00:32.760
Republicans have known for a long time that
when people learn critical thinking and epistemology,

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:35.660
they are way less likely to vote Republican.

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:40.680
So they've rooted out those subjects from
public school curricula in many States where

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:44.010
they've taken control of the boards of education.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:49.510
But there is an element of this that goes
beyond simply anti intellectual realism, which

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:55.380
is that trolling has become considered legitimate
political discourse.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:01:00.370
Yes, xenophobic talking points are considered
political discourse under Trump.

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:01.370
Yes.

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:05.040
The stuff that people like Ben Shapiro and
Charley cork do it.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:10.780
College is considered political discourse
now, but straight up trolling is also considered

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:12.220
political discourse now.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:17.470
So trolling, just so we can define it, trolling
is deliberately posting or saying the most

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:22.700
inflammatory, extraneous or off topic thing
that you can in a discussion.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:26.770
So rather than participating in the discussion
in good faith, you're effectively derailing

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:31.190
the conversation, sowing discord, trying to
cause disagreements or arguments or even just

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:34.479
getting people to give up, participating in
the conversation altogether.

00:01:34.479 --> 00:01:39.440
And there's a really good piece in wired by
Jonathan Albright who says, trolling is now

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:42.060
considered mainstream political discourse.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:47.380
The Overton window of acceptable behavior
in political conversations and discourse has

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:52.420
been widened to include as if it were legitimate
over trolling.

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:56.400
There's a really good study of social media
messages which finds that over time, more

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:02.140
and more the message is posted about political
issues and about candidates are messages that

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:08.270
are sort of a mix of hate speech, harassment,
amplifying unproven rumors and conspiracies,

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:13.420
spreading fake news, swarming and dog piling
people and just direct attacks, character

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:15.529
assassinations and insults.

00:02:15.529 --> 00:02:20.719
The number of comments about politics that
are meant to inspire action like voting or

00:02:20.719 --> 00:02:25.310
activism, contacting elected officials, those
are diminishing more and more.

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:27.180
Now, this is true on the left and right.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:31.299
We don't have to argue in what proportion
the right is much more a purveyor of fake

00:02:31.299 --> 00:02:34.290
news, much more a purveyor of conspiracy theories.

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:35.659
There's no question about that.

00:02:35.659 --> 00:02:40.530
The trolling is mixed, but we don't have to
debate who does it more because it hurts the

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:41.530
left more.

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:42.629
That's the reality.

00:02:42.629 --> 00:02:48.400
The right winds, when fewer people vote, the
right winds, when fewer people are engaged,

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:53.749
if more and more of the discussion is the
type that makes people like voting is pointless,

00:02:53.749 --> 00:02:58.519
like their voices are relevant, that they
become frustrated and they bail out the right

00:02:58.519 --> 00:02:59.519
winds.

00:02:59.519 --> 00:03:03.450
When the algorithms and the filter bubbles
keep people in their own circles except for

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:08.859
vicious attacks and hate speech and insults
so that people do nothing instead of be active.

00:03:08.859 --> 00:03:11.739
So they watch Netflix instead of be politically
active.

00:03:11.739 --> 00:03:18.230
The oligarchs and the billionaire class get
their way because others are bailing out.

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:20.540
That's why I say we're at a disadvantage.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:24.650
We want to do things and they want to keep
things the same.

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:27.199
We want to do things.

00:03:27.199 --> 00:03:32.930
They want no one to get involved so that they
can basically maintain the status quo while

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:40.279
perpetuating the inequality cycles that we've
been seeing by creating gridlock and despair

00:03:40.279 --> 00:03:42.040
and apathy.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:43.040
They win.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.680
So we have to overcome that and we have to
do stuff.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:50.959
And this is one of the ways, one of the ways
in which the Trump era will have destructive

00:03:50.959 --> 00:03:55.719
effects well beyond his presidency, however
long it ends up lasting earlier we dealt with

00:03:55.719 --> 00:03:59.959
some of the other ways, uh, that Donald Trump
is going to have a destructive effect on the

00:03:59.959 --> 00:04:02.480
United States, well beyond his presidency.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:07.779
This is one of those ways and it is that trolling
and all sorts of other horrible stuff are

00:04:07.779 --> 00:04:12.420
actually considered political discourse and
a way to discuss political issues and figure

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:17.709
out who we want to vote for that is extraordinarily
destructive and one of the great insulators

00:04:17.709 --> 00:04:21.819
against this there, there, there are many,
but one of the great insulators against this

00:04:21.819 --> 00:04:27.060
is a more educated population of population
who understands critical thinking and epistemology.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:32.199
As we talked about yesterday and that segment
interviewing a group of uneducated white swing

00:04:32.199 --> 00:04:33.979
state female voters.

00:04:33.979 --> 00:04:35.300
Go back and check that one out if you missed
it.

